// @title   TropeLang Concept Library — Narrative
// @version 1.3
// @note    Concepts that operate at the level of story structure — what stories
//          are made of, what drives them, and what they resolve.
//          These are the building blocks of plot and theme across all genres.
//
// Import: import "trl/concepts/narrative.trl"


// =============================================================================
// THE TELLING — the literal frame around a story
// The metafictional participants and the story-as-artifact. Promoted to the
// concept layer so any trope (rhetoric, metafiction, paratext) names one shared node.
// =============================================================================

// The literal story observer — the one the telling is for (reader, viewer, player).
concept audience       [%Narrative]   // the one whose attention the telling is built around

// The literal writer — the maker behind the telling, outside the story's own world.
concept author         [%Narrative]   // the one playing god over the telling

// The lesson a story imparts — its didactic takeaway, the point the events add up to.
concept moral          [%Timeless] [+Abstract]

// The structured sequence of events a story tells — its causal spine, distinct from
// the raw chronology (fabula) and from theme.
concept plot           [%Narrative] [+Abstract] [+Generative]


// =============================================================================
// OBLIGATION AND CALLING
// =============================================================================

// An obligation arising from one's role, covenant, or circumstance.
// Duty may conflict with desire, survival, or other duties; the conflict is generative.
concept duty           [%Timeless] [+Binding] [+Abstract]

// A strong sense of being summoned toward a particular purpose or action.
// Calling is experienced as external to self, even when it originates within.
concept calling        [%Timeless] [+Abstract]

// An obligation arising from a completed act of giving or help received.
// A favor creates an implicit debt; its repayment closes the loop.
concept favor          [%Timeless] [+Binding] [+Abstract]

// The state of being answerable; the willingness to bear the weight of one's choices.
// Responsibility is both a burden and a mark of agency.
concept responsibility [%Timeless] [+Binding] [+Abstract]


// =============================================================================
// CONFLICT AND OPPOSITION
// =============================================================================

// The state in which two or more forces, desires, or parties are in opposition.
// Conflict is the engine of narrative; without it, story collapses into description.
concept conflict       [%Timeless] [+Generative] [+Abstract]

// A condition in which two legitimate goods or duties are mutually exclusive.
// Dilemmas cannot be resolved without cost; they reveal character.
concept dilemma        [%Timeless] [+Abstract] [+Paradoxical]

// The desire to inflict suffering on one who has caused harm; retributive justice felt personally.
// Revenge differs from justice in that it is personal, emotional, and often cyclical.
concept revenge        [%Timeless] [+Corrupting] [+Abstract]

// The forgiveness extended to one who has harmed; the release of a grievance.
// Mercy toward an offender — distinct from general mercy in its specificity.
concept pardon         [%Timeless] [+Aspirational] [+Abstract]


// =============================================================================
// TRANSFORMATION AND ARC
// =============================================================================

// The movement from a negative or degraded condition toward restoration or improvement.
// Redemption requires acknowledgment of prior falling; it cannot begin in innocence.
concept redemption     [%Timeless] [+Aspirational] [+Abstract]

// The movement from a positive or neutral condition toward moral or practical degradation.
// Corruption is gradual; rarely does an entity fall all at once.
concept corruption     [%Timeless] [+Corrupting] [+Abstract]

// The voluntary acceptance of cost or loss for the benefit of another or a larger whole.
// The value of sacrifice is partly constituted by what is given up.
concept sacrifice      [%Timeless] [+Abstract]

// The condition of giving in — accepting defeat, abandoning resistance, or yielding.
// Surrender may be pragmatic, dignified, humiliating, or liberating.
concept surrender      [%Timeless] [+Abstract]

// The state of having been fundamentally altered by experience.
// To be changed is not the same as to be transformed — transformation is irreversible.
concept transformation [%Timeless] [+Irreversible] [+Abstract]

// The return to a prior state or position after a departure.
// Return is never truly a return to the same — both the entity and the context have changed.
concept return         [%Timeless] [+Abstract] [+Paradoxical]


// =============================================================================
// KNOWLEDGE AND REVELATION
// =============================================================================

// The sudden or gradual disclosure of what was concealed.
// Revelation reorganizes the meaning of prior events retroactively.
concept revelation     [%Timeless] [+Irreversible] [+Abstract]

// The concealment of relevant information from those who have a stake in it.
// Secrets create asymmetry of knowledge; their revelation reorganizes power.
concept secret         [%Timeless] [+Abstract]

// A prior event or sign that anticipates a later one without specifying it.
// Foreshadowing creates expectation; its fulfillment or subversion produces meaning.
concept foreshadowing  [%Timeless] [+Abstract]

// What an entity leaves behind after it ends; the ongoing effects of its existence.
// Legacy outlasts the entity and is shaped by those who remain and remember.
concept legacy         [%Timeless] [+Abstract]

// The record of what has occurred — distinct from memory (personal) and history (collective).
// The past is fixed; its interpretation is not.
concept the_past       [%Timeless] [+Irreversible] [+Abstract]

// The domain of what has not yet occurred; possibility not yet actualized.
// The future is open; its apparent closure is always provisional.
concept the_future     [%Timeless] [+Abstract]


// =============================================================================
// COST AND CONSEQUENCE
// =============================================================================

// The principle that actions produce effects that fall on the actor or others.
// Consequence may be immediate, delayed, proportional, or disproportionate.
concept consequence    [%Timeless] [+Inevitable] [+Abstract]

// The toll that achievement, survival, or growth extracts from the entity.
// Cost may be accepted, hidden, or discovered only in retrospect.
concept cost           [%Timeless] [+Inevitable] [+Abstract]

// The condition of being unable to proceed without paying what is owed.
// A price is the cost attached to a specific good or action.
concept price          [%Timeless] [+Binding] [+Abstract]

// An event or action that cannot be taken back; one whose effects are permanent.
// The point of no return is the moment before which reversal was possible.
concept point_of_no_return [%Timeless] [+Irreversible] [+Abstract]
